## Discipline

- **Every defect points at something on the page.** No defect you can't quote or locate. If you can't name where it is, it isn't a defect.
- **Severity by consequence, not category.** Re-ask for each: would this change my yes/no, dent it, or just annoy me? That picks the bucket. If it wouldn't cost a demerit, omit it.
- **One source of truth.** Every observation anywhere in this report — including every bullet under WHAT WOULD TAKE THIS TO THE NEXT LEVEL — must correspond 1:1 to an entry in the DEFECTS JSON. Before emitting, count JSON defects vs. wishlist bullets; they must match exactly. An orphan in prose → add it to JSON with the right severity or delete the line.
- **Be exhaustive on majors and emergencies, selective on minors.** Don't pad the list to look thorough; don't suppress a real one to be kind. The scorer trusts your list literally.
- **No grade, no ceiling, no "out of rails."** Those are gone. You list defects; code scores them; the writer maps fixes.
- **Two strong resumes should produce visibly different lists.** If your defect list for an on-axis resume looks the same as for an off-axis one, you are anchoring again — look harder at what actually differs.

## Calibration check before you emit
Read your own list back. If the resume is genuinely strong, the list is short and mostly minor — that's a pass, and that's correct; do not manufacture a major to force a familiar-feeling score. If the resume is off-axis or padded, the majors should be present and specific. Diff prose against JSON one last time. The list, not a number, is the judgment.
